\begin_layout Title
Local Government Consolidation: Is There Value in Downsizing?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
October 2, 2013
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Since the most recent recession, many local governments across the country
 have been struggling to eliminate debt or prevent bankrupcy.
 Residents complain that taxes are too high but then wince at cuts to services.
 Today, all sorts of reforms to local government are being proposed.
 As municipalities emerge from the recession, consolidation and streamlining
 continue to be a discussion as cities begin their budget season.
 Yet, consolidating local government departments is one of the trickiest
 subjects facing local leaders.
 Government fragmentation has long been torn between two competing interests.
 On one hand, many officials argue consolidation boosts efficiency and makes
 coordination easier.
 Citizens, though, remain attached to their local governments and often
 resent representation by a perceived group of outsiders.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

With my research project, I hope to determine if consolidating local government
 departments is an effective way of cutting costs.
 To do this, I intend to employ a difference-in-difference strategy.
 My analysis will focus on the cities nationwide that have consolidated
 local government departments with those overseen by the respective county.
 In fact, I hope to conduct two separate difference-in-difference examinations
 at both the city and county level with this setup.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The treatment group in the first configuration will be any municipality
 that has consolidated its government service with its county's, while the
 control group has elected against the measure.
 My identifying assumption is that absent the treatment, the change in costs
 to the treatment and control cities would have been the same over time.
 I can expect this assumption to hold because within a county, I do not
 expect there to be drastic systematic differences between cities.
 Also, no other time-varying factor should affect my estimates.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

With the next arrangement, I propose to investigate county differences in
 costs.
 Like before, the treatment group will consist of any county that agreed
 to consolidate its services with any of the cities located in its borders.
 On the other hand, the control group will contain directly adjacent counties
 that have chosen not to consolidate their services.
 My identifying assumption is that absent the treatment, the change in costs
 to the treatment and control counties would have been the same over time.
 In order for the identifying assumption to hold, I will disregard counties
 that contain excessively large municipalities, i.e., Los Angeles County or
 Miami Dade County.
 Instead, I will limit my attention to counties with much smaller local
 governments, such as Brazos County.
 Thus, I do not expect there to be any major systematic differences between
 the small, adjacent counties of the treatment and control groups when using
 this setup.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For this project, I intend to use Census of Governments data and figures
 over the last several periods.
 The Census Bureau conducts the Census of Governments every five years,
 assessing organizational types, employment and finances for all state and
 local governments.
\end_layout

